(customers,"[144-3] they are entirely unwarranted. No man who manages his) Tj T*
(own affairs well, or those of a household, lives on the capital or) Tj T*
(income of another man; but every one lives on his own income, by the) Tj T*
(things he has himself produced; although with every further development) Tj T*
(of the division of labor, it becomes rarer that any one puts the) Tj T*
(finishing stroke to his own products, and can satisfy himself by their) Tj T*
(immediate consumption alone. Hence we should call nothing diverted or) Tj T*
(derived income except that which has been gratuitously obtained from) Tj T*
(another.[144-4]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 144-1: Including of course, gifts, inheritances,) Tj T*
(     lottery prizes, etc.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 144-2: Thus the original income of the peasant) Tj T*
(     consists in his corn, of the miller in his flour, of the) Tj T*
(     baker in his bread, of the shoemaker in his shoes. The money) Tj T*
(     which circulates among all these and the purchaser, is only) Tj T*
(     the means of exchanging that part of their products which) Tj T*
(     they cannot themselves use, for other goods. Money, on the) Tj T*
(     other hand, was the original income of the producers of the) Tj T*
(     gold or silver it contains. Compare _Mirabeau_, Philosophie) Tj T*
(     rurale, 1763, ch. 3. _Adam Smith_, II, ch. 2. But) Tj T*
(     especially, see _J. B. Say_, Traité II, ch. 1, 5; and) Tj T*
(     _Sismondi_, N. P., I, 90, 376, in which it is correctly) Tj T*
(     said, that the quality which constitutes anything capital or) Tj T*
(     income does not inhere in the thing itself, but depends on) Tj T*
(     the person. Compare, however, I, 148; _Hermann_, Staatsw.) Tj T*
(     Untersuch. 297 ff., 33 seq.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 144-3: A fundamental thought in _St. Chamans_, Du) Tj T*
(     Système d'Impôt, 1820. Nouvel Essai sur la Richesse des) Tj T*
(     Nations, 1824.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 144-4: Thus, for instance, the support given by) Tj T*
(     the head of a family to the members thereof; also gifts,) Tj T*
(     alms, thefts. Even _A. L. Schlözer_, St. A., II, 487, will) Tj T*
(     allow that no one "eats the bread of another," but the) Tj T*
(     person who has received it from the latter by way of favor) Tj T*
(     and for nothing. In the case of a rented house, there is) Tj T*
(     only an exchange of objects of income. The person to whom it) Tj T*
(     is rented gives up a portion of his, and the renting party) Tj T*
(     the use of his house. Similarly, in the case of personal) Tj T*
(     services. Writers who maintain that only certain kinds of) Tj T*
(     useful labor are productive, must of course extend the) Tj T*
(     limits of diverted income much farther. See _Lotz_,) Tj T*
(     Handbuch, III, § 133; _Rau_, Lehrbuch, I, §§ 248, 251.) Tj T*
(     _Cantillon_ thinks that if no landowner spent more than his) Tj T*
(     income, it would be scarcely possible for any one else to) Tj T*
(     grow rich. \(Nature du Commerce, 75.\) According to _Stein_,) Tj T*
(     Lehrbuch, 347, every one gets his income from the income of) Tj T*
