() Tj T*
(SECTION CXLV.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(INCOME.--GROSS, FREE AND NET.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In all _income_, we may distinguish a _gross_ amount, a _net_ amount and) Tj T*
(a _free_ amount.[145-1] The gross income of a year, for instance,) Tj T*
(consists of all the goods which have been newly produced within that) Tj T*
(time. The net[145-2] income is that portion of the former which remains) Tj T*
(after deducting the cost of production \(§ 106\), and which may therefore) Tj T*
(be consumed without diminishing the original resources. Only the new) Tj T*
(values incorporated in the new commodities make up the net income.) Tj T*
(Evidently, a great portion of what is considered in one business the) Tj T*
(cost of production is net income in a great many others; as for) Tj T*
(instance, what the person engaged in one enterprise in production has) Tj T*
(paid out in wages and interest on capital. By means of this outlay, a) Tj T*
(portion of his circulating capital is drawn by others as income, and, on) Tj T*
(the other hand, a portion of their original income is turned into a) Tj T*
(portion of his circulating capital.[145-3] _Free_ income, I call that) Tj T*
(portion of net income which remains available to the producer after his) Tj T*
(indispensable wants have been satisfied.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An accurate kind of book-keeping which keeps these three elements of) Tj T*
(income separate is more generally practicable as civilization advances.) Tj T*
(We might call it the _economic balance_. Where commerce is very thriving) Tj T*
(it is even customary to provide by law that those classes who need it) Tj T*
(especially should have this species of book-keeping. People in a lower) Tj T*
(stage of cultivation, with their poetical nature, are unfriendly to such) Tj T*
(calculations.[145-4] [145-5] And where natural-economy \(_Naturalwirthschaft_\)) Tj T*
(or barter prevails, a book-keeping of this kind of any accuracy is scarcely) Tj T*
(practicable. The ratio which net income bears to gross income is a very) Tj T*
(important element to enable us to judge of the advantageousness of any) Tj T*
(method of production. If every producer should succeed in consequence of) Tj T*
(keeping his books in this manner, in determining exactly the cost to him of) Tj T*
(each of his products, this would be an economic progress similar to that of) Tj T*
(general spread of good chemical knowledge in the arts. On the amount of) Tj T*
(_free_ income, on the other hand, depends all the higher enjoyment of life,) Tj T*
(all rational beneficence, and the progressive enrichment of mankind.[145-6]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 145-1: Similarly in _Sismondi_, N. P., II, 330,) Tj T*
(     and _Rau_, Lehrbuch, I, § 71, a.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 145-2: Called by _Hermann_, loc. cit., simply) Tj T*
(     income.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 145-3: This truth _J. B. Say_ has exaggerated to) Tj T*
(     the extent of claiming that gross and net income are one and) Tj T*
(     the same so far as entire nations are concerned. \(Traité,) Tj T*
(     II, ch. 5; Cours pratique, III, 14; IV, 74.\) But the gross) Tj T*
(     profit of the entire production of any one year is much) Tj T*
(     greater than the simultaneous net income of all the) Tj T*
(     individuals engaged in it. This is accounted for by the fact) Tj T*
