(SECTION CXLVII.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(NATIONAL INCOME.--ITS STATISTICAL IMPORTANCE.) Tj T*
([CONTINUED.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The question frequently discussed, whether it is more advantageous to) Tj T*
(increase the gross income or the net income[147-1] of a people, may be) Tj T*
(readily answered with the assistance of our tripartite division. Since) Tj T*
(economic production has no other object than the satisfaction of human) Tj T*
(wants, the mere increase of the gross income of a people is a matter of) Tj T*
(indifference. An increase of the net income puts a people in a condition) Tj T*
(to increase either their numbers or their enjoyments. \(See §§ 163 and) Tj T*
(239.\) The most desirable condition is where both these results are) Tj T*
(produced. It is fortunate for a people when the _free_ income of the) Tj T*
(nation increases by reason of the absolute or relative decrease of the) Tj T*
(cost of production, which adds nothing to enjoyment. But it is) Tj T*
(politically and morally to be lamented when it increases at the expense) Tj T*
(of the satisfaction of man's necessary wants, especially if the majority) Tj T*
(of the people deny themselves in this respect to produce that end. Sir) Tj T*
(Thomas More called the sheep of his time, to make place for which so) Tj T*
(many farm houses were razed to the ground, ravenous beasts, which) Tj T*
(devoured men and laid waste city and country.[147-2]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 147-1: The greater number of writers, at bottom,) Tj T*
(     understand by this question only whether greater efforts) Tj T*
(     should be made to increase the wages of the lower classes or) Tj T*
(     the rent and rate of interest on capital paid to the higher.) Tj T*
(     \(_Schmoller_, in the Tüb. Zeitschrift, 1863, 22.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
