(SECTION CXLVIII.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE TWO PHASES OF INCOME.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In every income which has anything to do with other incomes, it is) Tj T*
(necessary to distinguish its immediately productive side, and its profit) Tj T*
(or acquisition side. It is necessary, in the first place, that all the) Tj T*
(products made by private parties should, so to speak, be put into the) Tj T*
(common treasury of the national economy, and that each should thence) Tj T*
(draw his own private revenue. Justice requires that there should be a) Tj T*
(perfect correlation between the two; that each should enjoy precisely) Tj T*
(the quota of the national income to the production of which his person) Tj T*
(or his property contributed. A just appreciation of the relative) Tj T*
(productive power of the divers branches of labor constitutes one of the) Tj T*
(chief bulwarks against the inroads of destructive socialistic theories.) Tj T*
(The person who calls a good doctor or a good judge unproductive should,) Tj T*
(to be consistent, call those who by their greater intelligence are) Tj T*
(fitted to superintend agricultural and industrial enterprises) Tj T*
(unproductive, also, as is done by the coarser socialists with their) Tj T*
(apotheosis of mere manual labor. Unfortunately, such a settlement as is) Tj T*
(above contemplated among the different factors of production, whose) Tj T*
(owners are desirous to divide the common product among them, is possible) Tj T*
(only where the factors of production are either of the same kind, or can) Tj T*
(be reduced to a common denominator.[148-1] But if justice pure and) Tj T*
(simple were meted out, no man could subsist. Love or charity must) Tj T*
(supplement justice in order to assist those \(and especially such as) Tj T*
(without any fault of theirs\) who are not able to produce anything, or) Tj T*
(enough to supply those wants, for instance, children and the poor.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As the net national income, following the three great factors of all) Tj T*
(economic production, is divided into three great branches, rent, wages) Tj T*
(and interest on capital, the net income from any private business may be) Tj T*
(reduced to one or more of these branches.[148-2] The three great) Tj T*
(branches of income may be considered with advantage from a great many) Tj T*
(different points of view. We may inquire in the case of each of them:) Tj T*
(concerning its absolute magnitude, its relation to the aggregate) Tj T*
(national income, to the magnitude of the factor of production, of which) Tj T*
(it constitutes the remuneration; by what number of men it is shared, and) Tj T*
