(From what we have said, it follows that the rent of the land of a) Tj T*
(country is equal at least to the sum of all the differences between the) Tj T*
(product of the least productive portions of capital which have been) Tj T*
(necessarily laid out in the cultivation of the soil and the product of) Tj T*
(the other portions more productively laid out by other husbandmen. It) Tj T*
(may rise higher than this on account of a coalition among landowners or) Tj T*
(immoderate competition among farmers, who may thereby be forced to) Tj T*
(surrender a portion of their wages and interest on capital to the) Tj T*
(former; but it can never lastingly fall below this amount. If the) Tj T*
(landowners themselves were to surrender all claim to rent, the price of) Tj T*
(agricultural products would not sink if the market was kept fully) Tj T*
(supplied; and the excess obtained from the better land over and above) Tj T*
(the cost of production would go, but only in the nature of a gift, to) Tj T*
(the farmers, corn dealers and individual consumers.[152-1] Normal rent) Tj T*
(is not to be explained by any mysterious or peculiar productiveness[152-2]) Tj T*
(of the land that yields it, but on the contrary, by the fact that even) Tj T*
(material forces unexhaustible in themselves, but which can be productive) Tj T*
(only in combination with given parcels of land, uniformly oppose even) Tj T*
(successively greater difficulties to every successive and additional) Tj T*
(improvement.[152-3]) Tj T*
