(SECTION CLVI.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(INFLUENCE OF ADVANCING CIVILIZATION ON RENT.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Advancing civilization contributes in three different ways to raise) Tj T*
(rents.[156-1] The growth of population necessitates either a more) Tj T*
(_intensive_ agriculture \(higher farming\), or causes it to extend over) Tj T*
(less fertile parcels of land, or parcels less advantageously) Tj T*
(situated.[156-2] If the growth of population be attended by an increase) Tj T*
(of capital, this happens in a still higher degree. The people now) Tj T*
(consume, if not more, at least wheat of finer quality, more and better) Tj T*
(fed live stock; the consequence of which is, that the demands made on) Tj T*
(the land are increased. Lastly, if the population be gradually) Tj T*
(concentrated in large cities, this fact also must contribute to raise) Tj T*
(rents, because it requires a multitude of costly transportations of) Tj T*
(agricultural produce and so increases the cost of production \(up to the) Tj T*
(time of consumption\) on the less advantageously situated) Tj T*
(land.[156-3] [156-4]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As most of the symptoms of a higher civilization become apparent) Tj T*
(earliest, and in the most striking manner, in large cities, so also a) Tj T*
(rise in rents is first felt in them. The building of houses may be) Tj T*
(considered as the most _intensive_ of all cultivation of land and that) Tj T*
(which is most firmly fixed to the soil.[156-5] Rent has nowhere an) Tj T*
(unsurpassable maximum any more than a necessary minimum.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
