(SECTION CLVII.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(HISTORY OF RENT.--IMPROVEMENTS IN THE ART OF AGRICULTURE.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Improvements in the art of agriculture which are confined to individual) Tj T*
(husbandmen leave rent unaffected. They do not perceptibly lower the) Tj T*
(price of agricultural products, and only effect an increase of the) Tj T*
(reward of enterprise which is entirely personal to the more skillful) Tj T*
(producers and does not attach to the ground itself.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But how is it when these improvements become general throughout the) Tj T*
(country? If population and consumption remain unchanged, the supply of) Tj T*
(agricultural products will exceed the demand. This would compel farmers,) Tj T*
(if there be no avenue open to exports, to curtail their production. The) Tj T*
(least fertile and most disadvantageously situated parcels of land will) Tj T*
(be abandoned to a greater or less extent, and the least productive) Tj T*
(capital devoted to agriculture, withdrawn. In this way, rent goes down) Tj T*
(both relatively and absolutely, although the owners of land may be able) Tj T*
(to partially cover their loss by the gain which results to them as) Tj T*
(consumers and capitalists.[157-1] \(§ 186\). After a time, however, and as) Tj T*
(a consequence of the diminished price of corn, population and) Tj T*
(consumption will increase, and entail an extension of agriculture and a) Tj T*
(consequent rise in rents.[157-2] If it, relatively speaking, reaches the) Tj T*
(same point as before, it still is absolutely much greater than before.) Tj T*
(Let us suppose that there are three classes of land of equal extent in a) Tj T*
(country, which for an equal outlay of capital produce 100,000, 80,000) Tj T*
(and 70,000 bushels yearly. The rent of the land here would be equal to) Tj T*
(at least 40,000 bushels. If the yield of production now doubles, while) Tj T*
(the demand for agricultural products also doubles, the aggregate harvest) Tj T*
(will be 200,000 + 160,000 + 140,000 bushels, and consequently rent will) Tj T*
(have risen to at least 80,000 bushels. But this increase of rent has) Tj T*
(injured no one. If the population increases in a less degree than the) Tj T*
(productiveness of the land, the consumer may, to a certain extent, gain) Tj T*
(largely, and the landowner better his condition. However, great) Tj T*
(agricultural improvements spread so gradually over a country, that, as a) Tj T*
(rule, the demand for agricultural products can keep pace with the) Tj T*
(increased supply. But even in this case, that transitory absolute) Tj T*
(decline of rent may be avoided; and it cannot be claimed universally, as) Tj T*
(it is by many who are satisfied with mumbling Ricardo's words after him,) Tj T*
(that an increase of rent is possible only by an enhancement of the price) Tj T*
(of the products of the soil. Where the development of a people's economy) Tj T*
(is a normal one, the rent of land is wont to increase gradually, but at) Tj T*
(the same time to constitute a diminishing quota of the entire national) Tj T*
(income.[157-3]) Tj T*
