(SECTION CLIX.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(HISTORY OF RENT.--RENT AND THE GENERAL GOOD.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We so frequently hear rent called the result of the monopoly[159-1] of) Tj T*
(land, and an undeserved tribute paid by the whole people to landowners,) Tj T*
(that it is high time we should call attention to the common advantage it) Tj T*
(is to all. There is evidently danger that, with the rapid growth of) Tj T*
(population, the mass of mankind should yield to the temptation of) Tj T*
(gradually confining themselves to the satisfaction of coarse, palpable) Tj T*
(wants; that all refined leisure, which makes life and the troubles that) Tj T*
(attend it worth enduring, and which is the indispensable foundation of) Tj T*
(all permanent progress and all higher activity, should be gradually) Tj T*
(surrendered. \(See § 145.\) Here rent constitutes a species of reserve) Tj T*
(fund, which grows greater in proportion as these dangers impend by) Tj T*
(reason of the decline of wages and of the profit of capital, or) Tj T*
(interest.[159-2] Besides, precisely in times when rent is high, the sale) Tj T*
(and divisibility of landed estates act as a beneficent reaction against) Tj T*
(the monopoly of land, which is always akin to the condition of things) Tj T*
(created by rent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But it is of immeasurably greater importance that high rents deter the) Tj T*
(people from abusing the soil in an anti-economic way; that they compel) Tj T*
(men to settle about the centers of commerce, to improve the means of) Tj T*
(transportation, and under certain circumstances to engage in the work of) Tj T*
(colonization; while, otherwise, idleness would soon reconcile itself to) Tj T*
(the heaping together of large swarms of men.[159-3] The anticipation of) Tj T*
(rent may render possible the construction of railroads, which enable the) Tj T*
(land to yield that very anticipated rent.) Tj T*
