(As a rule, a war over prices, carried on by such means, and without) Tj T*
(force on either side, must generally issue in the victory of the richer) Tj T*
(purchasers of labor.[176-3] The latter require the uninterrupted) Tj T*
(continuation of labor for their convenience and profit; but the workmen) Tj T*
(need it to live. It is but seldom that the workmen will be in a) Tj T*
(condition to stop work for more than a few months, without feeling the) Tj T*
(sting of hunger. The purchaser of labor can live longer on his capital;) Tj T*
(and the victory here belongs to the party who, in the struggle, holds) Tj T*
(out longest. Hence, a strike that lasts more than six weeks may, for) Tj T*
(that reason alone, be considered a failure. The employers of labor, on) Tj T*
(account of their smaller number and greater education, make their) Tj T*
(counter-coalition much more secret and effective. How many instances) Tj T*
(there are in which labor-saving machines have come into use more rapidly) Tj T*
(than they otherwise would have come but for the influence of these) Tj T*
(coalitions![176-4]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the other hand, it cannot be ignored that a host of workmen, by means) Tj T*
(of an organization which provides them with a unity of will, such as the) Tj T*
(heads of great enterprises naturally possess, must become much better) Tj T*
(skilled in carrying on a struggle for higher wages. Where wages in) Tj T*
(general tend to rise, but by force of custom, which is specially) Tj T*
(powerful here \(§ 170\), are kept below their natural level, a strike may) Tj T*
(very soon attain its end. And workmen are all the more to be wished) Tj T*
(God-speed here in proportion as employers are slow to decide of their) Tj T*
(own motion upon raising wages, and where, under certain) Tj T*
(circumstances,[176-5] a single cold-hearted master might force all his) Tj T*
(competitors to keep wages down. If even the entire working class should) Tj T*
(follow the example of the strikers, so that all commodities, in so far) Tj T*
(as they are products of labor, should grow dearer to an extent) Tj T*
(corresponding to the rise in wages, there would still remain an) Tj T*
(improvement of the condition of the working class at the cost of the) Tj T*
(interest paid on capital and the profits of enterprise. It is, of) Tj T*
(course, otherwise with the struggle of workmen against the natural) Tj T*
(conditions which determine the rate of their wages \(§§ 161-166\) in which) Tj T*
(they might, in turbulent times, possibly succeed[176-6] temporarily, but) Tj T*
(would, in the long run, have to fail.[176-7]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The working class will be best fortified in such a struggle for higher) Tj T*
(wages when their organization is a permanent one, and when they have) Tj T*
(taken care, during good times, to collect a certain amount of capital to) Tj T*
(protect their members, during their cessation from work, against acute) Tj T*
(want. This is the object of the trades-unions as they have grown up in) Tj T*
(England, especially since the total decline of the guild system and of) Tj T*
(governmental provisions relating to apprentices, fixed rates of) Tj T*
(wages[176-8] etc. But it cannot be denied that these unions, although) Tj T*
(democratic in form, often exercise a very despotic sway over their) Tj T*
(members;[176-9] that they have, so far as the employers of labor are) Tj T*
(concerned, and the non-union laborers, gone back to a number of) Tj T*
(measures, outgrowths of the guild and embargo systems, which it was) Tj T*
(fondly hoped had been forever banished by the freedom of) Tj T*
(industry.[176-10] What many of the friends of this system hope it may) Tj T*
(accomplish in the future, viz.: regulate the whole relation between) Tj T*
