(HISTORY OF THE RATE OF INTEREST.--INFLUENCE OF AN ADVANCE IN) Tj T*
(CIVILIZATION.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(With an advance in civilization, the rate of interest is wont to) Tj T*
(decline.[185-1] [185-2] One of the chief causes of this phenomenon is the) Tj T*
(necessity, as population and consumption increase, to employ capital in) Tj T*
(the fertilization of less productive land, and in less profitable) Tj T*
(investments.[185-3] An increase in the stock of money does not) Tj T*
(necessarily depreciate the rate of interest. If this increase comes in) Tj T*
(connection with a corresponding depreciation of the individual pieces of) Tj T*
(metal, it cannot be said that the nation has thereby become richer in) Tj T*
(capital. All that would be required in such case is only a greater) Tj T*
(number of pounds of gold or silver, or more paper bills to represent the) Tj T*
(same capital.[185-4] Only during the transition-period, during which the) Tj T*
(depreciation of money is still incomplete, is the rate of interest wont) Tj T*
(to be lowered; and all the more, since loaned capital is generally) Tj T*
(offered and sought after in the form of money.[185-5] [185-6]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The decline of the rate of interest generally shows itself earliest in) Tj T*
(the large cities, which are everywhere the national organ, in which the) Tj T*
(good and bad symptoms of later civilization may be soonest) Tj T*
(observed.[185-7]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Moreover, the condition of capitalists is not necessarily made worse by) Tj T*
(a decline of the rate of interest. It is possible that, for a long time,) Tj T*
(the increase of capital should continue more rapid than the decrease of) Tj T*
(interest for each individual. \(If, indeed, the aggregate interest of) Tj T*
(capital should become absolutely smaller, there is always a pleasant) Tj T*
