(The canon law, from the first, endeavored to prevent contracts for) Tj T*
(interest. We may even say that the prohibition of interest-usury is the) Tj T*
(key-stone of the whole system of the political economy of the _Corpus) Tj T*
(Juris Canonici_. The development of that law coincides, as to time, with) Tj T*
(the senility of the Roman Empire and the childhood of modern) Tj T*
(nations.[191-1] In the golden age of papal power, every) Tj T*
(interest-creditor was refused the communion, the _testamenti factio_ and) Tj T*
(the right of ecclesiastical burial. Proceedings at law could not be) Tj T*
(instituted for the recovery of the principal debt until the creditor had) Tj T*
(restored all the interest obtained. In the council of Vienna, in 1311,) Tj T*
(it was declared heresy to defend the taking of interest. The universal) Tj T*
(antipathy of the church towards the growing importance of the) Tj T*
(_bourgeoisie_,[191-2] and the desire to give the spiritual courts an) Tj T*
(extensive jurisdiction in litigated cases, may have contributed largely) Tj T*
(to the adoption of these measures. In later medieval times, the secular) Tj T*
(power offered its services to execute these laws;[191-3] and, to judge) Tj T*
(of what public opinion in this matter was, we need only call to mind the) Tj T*
(decided disapproval of interest by Dante, Luther and Shakespeare.[191-4]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
