(SECTION CXCVI \(_a._\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(UNDERTAKER'S PROFIT.--HAVING THE "LEAD.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The undertaker's profit is that branch of the national income in which) Tj T*
(the greater number of new fortunes are made. If a landowner has a large) Tj T*
(income, he generally considers himself obliged to make a correspondingly) Tj T*
(large outlay, one in keeping with his position; and workmen who are not) Tj T*
(undertakers themselves seldom have the means to make large savings.) Tj T*
(Besides, undertakers stand between the purchasers of their products and) Tj T*
(the lessors of the productive forces used by them in the peculiarly) Tj T*
(favorable situation which I may describe by the expression: having, as) Tj T*
(they say in card-playing, "the lead."[196a-1] When, in the struggle for) Tj T*
(prices, one party occupies a position which enables him to observe every) Tj T*
(change of circumstance much sooner than his opponent, the latter may) Tj T*
(always suffer from the effects of erroneous prices. If, for instance,) Tj T*
(the productiveness of business increases, even without any personal) Tj T*
(merit of the individual undertakers themselves, it will always be some) Tj T*
(time before the decline in the price of commodities and the rise in the) Tj T*
(rate of interest take place, as a result of the increased competition of) Tj T*
(undertakers, consequent upon the extraordinary rate of the undertaker's) Tj T*
(profit. It is difficult, and even impossible in most instances for the) Tj T*
