(Hence a harmony of the large, medium and small incomes may be considered) Tj T*
(the indispensable condition of the economic prosperity of a) Tj T*
(people.[205-1] This prosperity is best secured when the medium-class) Tj T*
(income prevails, when no citizen is so rich that he can buy the others,) Tj T*
(and no one so poor that he might be compelled to sell himself. \(_J. J.) Tj T*
(Rousseau._\)[205-2] Where there is not a numerous class of citizens who) Tj T*
(have time enough to serve the state even gratis, as jurymen, overseers) Tj T*
(of the poor, municipal officers, representatives of the people etc.) Tj T*
(\(compare § 63\), and property enough to be independent of the whims and) Tj T*
(caprices of others, and to maintain themselves and the state in times of) Tj T*
(need, even the most excellent of constitutions must remain a dead) Tj T*
(letter. Nor should there be an entire absence of large fortunes, and) Tj T*
(even of inherited large fortunes. The changes of ministry which) Tj T*
(accompany constitutional government are fully possible only when the) Tj T*
(choice of men who would not lose their social position by a cessation of) Tj T*
(their salaries as public functionaries is not altogether too) Tj T*
(limited.[205-3] Thus the transaction of the most important political) Tj T*
(business, especially that which relates to foreign affairs, requires a) Tj T*
(peculiar elasticity of mind, and a capacity for routine on the grandest) Tj T*
(scale, which with very rare exceptions, can be acquired only by) Tj T*
(habituation to them from childhood, and which are lost as soon as the) Tj T*
(care for food is felt. The bird's-eye-view of those who are born "great") Tj T*
(does not, by any means, embrace the whole truth of human things, but it) Tj T*
(does a very important side of it. Among this class, as a rule, it is) Tj T*
(easiest to find great party leaders, while leaders who have to be paid) Tj T*
(by their party, generally become in the long run, mere party) Tj T*
(tools.[205-4] It is true that it requires great intellectual and moral) Tj T*
(power to resist the temptations which a brilliant hereditary condition) Tj T*
(presents; temptations especially to idleness, to pride and debauchery.) Tj T*
(For ordinary men, it is a moral and, in the end, an economic blessing,) Tj T*
(that they have to eat their bread in the sweat of their brow,[205-5] and) Tj T*
(that they can grow rich only by long-continued frugality.[205-6]) Tj T*
(However, the distribution of the national income, and every change in) Tj T*
(that same distribution, constitute one of the most important but at the) Tj T*
(same time one of the most obscure departments of statistics.[205-7] When) Tj T*
(inequality increases because the lower classes absolutely decline, there) Tj T*
(is no use in talking any longer about the prosperity of the) Tj T*
(nation.[205-8] It is different, of course, when only the higher classes) Tj T*
(become, relatively speaking, higher yet. But even this latter kind of) Tj T*
(inequality may operate disastrously, inasmuch as it nourishes the most) Tj T*
(dangerous tendency of democracy, that of envy towards those who are) Tj T*
(better off.) Tj T*
