(The greater number of such crises are doubtless special; that is, it is) Tj T*
(only in some branches of trade that supply outweighs demand. Most) Tj T*
(theorists deny the possibility of a general glut, although many) Tj T*
(practitioners stubbornly maintain it.[216-1] J. B. Say relies upon the) Tj T*
(principle that in the sale of products, as contradistinguished from) Tj T*
(gifts, inheritances, etc., payment can always be made only in other) Tj T*
(products. If, therefore, in one branch there be so much supplied that) Tj T*
(the price declines; as a matter of course, the commodity wanted in) Tj T*
(exchange will command all the more, and, therefore, have a better vent.) Tj T*
(In the years 1812 and 1813, for instance, it was almost impossible to) Tj T*
(find a market for dry goods and other similar products. Merchants) Tj T*
(everywhere complained that nothing could be sold. At the same time,) Tj T*
(however, corn, meat and colonial products were very dear, and,) Tj T*
(therefore, paid a large profit to those who supplied them.[216-2] Every) Tj T*
(producer who wants to sell anything brings a demand into the market) Tj T*
(exactly corresponding to his supply. \(_J. Mill._\) Every seller is _ex vi) Tj T*
(termini_ also a buyer; if, therefore production is doubled, purchasing) Tj T*
(power is also doubled. \(_J. S. Mill._\) Supply and demand are in the last) Tj T*
(analysis, really, only two different sides of one and the same) Tj T*
(transaction. And as long as we see men badly fed, badly clothed, etc.,) Tj T*
(so long, strictly speaking, shall we be scarcely able to say that too) Tj T*
(much food or too much clothing has been produced.[216-3]) Tj T*
