() Tj T*
(LIMITS TO THE SAVING OF CAPITAL.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It may be seen from the foregoing, that the mere saving of capital, if) Tj T*
(the nation is to be really enriched thereby, has its limits. Every) Tj T*
(consumer likes to extend his consumption-supply and his capital in use) Tj T*
(\(_Gebrauchskapitalien_\); but not beyond a certain point.[221-1] Besides,) Tj T*
(as trade becomes more flourishing, smaller stores answer the same) Tj T*
(purpose. And no intelligent man can desire his productive capital) Tj T*
(increased except up to the limit that he expects a larger market for his) Tj T*
(enlarged production. What merchant or manufacturer is there who would) Tj T*
(rejoice or consider himself enriched, if the number of his customers and) Tj T*
(their desire to purchase remaining the same, he saw his stores of) Tj T*
(unsaleable articles increase every year by several thousands?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This is another difference between national resources or world resources) Tj T*
(and private resources. The resources of a private person, which are only) Tj T*
(a link in the whole chain of trade, and which are, therefore, estimated) Tj T*
(at the value in exchange of their component parts should, indeed, always) Tj T*
(be increased by savings made. \(§ 8.\) For even the most excessive) Tj T*
(increase of supply in general, which largely lowers the price of a whole) Tj T*
(class of commodities, will never reduce the price of individual) Tj T*
(quantities of that commodity below zero, and scarcely to zero. It is) Tj T*
(quite otherwise in the case of national or world resources which must be) Tj T*
(estimated according to the value in use of their component parts. Every) Tj T*
(utility supposes a want. Where, therefore, the want of a commodity has) Tj T*
(not increased, and notwithstanding there is a continuing increase in the) Tj T*
(supply, the only result must be a corresponding decrease in the utility) Tj T*
(of each individual part.[221-2]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If a people were to save all that remained to them over and above their) Tj T*
(most urgent necessities, they would soon be obliged to seek a wider) Tj T*
(market in foreign countries, or loan their capital there; but they would) Tj T*
(make no advance whatever in higher culture nor add anything to the) Tj T*
(gladness of life.[221-3] On the other hand, if they would not save at) Tj T*
(all, they would be able to extend their enjoyments only at the expense) Tj T*
(of their capital and of their future. Yet these two extremes find their) Tj T*
(correctives in themselves. In the former case, a glut of the market) Tj T*
(would soon produce an increased consumption and a diminished production;) Tj T*
(in the latter the reverse. The ideal of progress demands that the) Tj T*
(increased outlay with increased production should be made only for) Tj T*
(worthy objects, and chiefly by the rich, while the middle and lower) Tj T*
(classes should continue to make savings and thus contribute to wipe out) Tj T*
(differences of fortune.[221-4]) Tj T*
