(As there are extravagant and frugal individuals, so also are there) Tj T*
(extravagant and frugal nations. Thus, for instance, we must ascribe) Tj T*
(great national frugality to the Swiss. In many well-to-do families in) Tj T*
(that country, it is a principle acted upon to require the daughters to) Tj T*
(look to the results of their white sewing, instead of giving them) Tj T*
(pin-money; to gather up the crumbs after coffee parties in the presence) Tj T*
(of the guests, and to make soup of them afterwards, etc. Sons are) Tj T*
(generally neither supported nor helped to any great extent by their) Tj T*
(parents in their lifetime, and are required to found their own homes.) Tj T*
(They, therefore, grow rich from inheritance only late in years, when) Tj T*
(they are accustomed to a retired and modest mode of life, and have) Tj T*
(little desire, from mere convenience sake, to change it for another. And) Tj T*
(so Temple informs us that it never occurs to the Dutch that their outlay) Tj T*
(should equal their income; and when this is the case they consider that) Tj T*
(they have spent the year in vain. Such a mode of life would cost a man) Tj T*
(his reputation there as much as vicious excess does in other countries.) Tj T*
(The greatest order and the most accurate calculation of all outlay in) Tj T*
(advance is found in union with this; so that Temple assures us he never) Tj T*
(heard of a public or private building which was not finished at the time) Tj T*
(stipulated for in advance.[222-1]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the other hand, the Englishman lives rather luxuriantly. He is so) Tj T*
(used to enjoying comparative abundance, that when English travelers see) Tj T*
(the peasantry of the continent living in great frugality, they generally) Tj T*
(attribute it to poverty and not to their disposition to make savings. If) Tj T*
(England has grown rich, it is because of the colossal magnitude of its) Tj T*
(production, which is still more luxuriant and abundant than its) Tj T*
(consumption.[222-2] This contrast may be the effect in part of) Tj T*
(nationality and climate;[222-3] but it is certainly the effect in part) Tj T*
(also of a difference in the stage of civilization which these countries) Tj T*
(have respectively reached. The elder Cato had a maxim that a widow) Tj T*
(might, indeed, allow her fortune to diminish, but that it was a man's) Tj T*
(duty to leave more behind him than he had inherited.[222-4] And how) Tj T*
(prodigally did not the lords of the universe live in later times!) Tj T*
