(The luxury of that uncivilized age shows itself for the most part on) Tj T*
(particular occasions, and then all the more ostentatious, while in the) Tj T*
(periods following it, it rather permeates the whole of life. Even J.) Tj T*
(Möser excuses our forefathers for their mad celebration of their) Tj T*
(_kirmesses_ and carnivals: _dulce est desipere in loco_, as Horace says,) Tj T*
(and that they sometimes carried it to the extent of drowning) Tj T*
(reason.[226-1] Among ourselves, the common man drinks brandy every day;) Tj T*
(in Russia, seldom, but then, to the greatest excess.[226-2] The well) Tj T*
(known peculiarity of feudal castles, that, besides one enormous hall,) Tj T*
(they were wont to have very small and inconvenient rooms for every day) Tj T*
(life, is accounted for in part by the great importance to them of festal) Tj T*
(occasions, and in part by the cordiality of the life led in them, in) Tj T*
(which lord and servants constituted one family. Nothing can be more) Tj T*
(erroneous than to ascribe great temperance in general to people in a low) Tj T*
(stage of civilization. Their simplicity is a consequence of their) Tj T*
(ignorance rather than of their self-control. When nomadic races have) Tj T*
(once tasted the cup of more delicate enjoyment, it is wont to hurry them) Tj T*
(to destruction.[226-3]) Tj T*
