(and the effeminate.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Rome, in the earlier part of the empire, affords us an example of such) Tj T*
(luxury on the most extensive scale.[232-1] Nero paid three hundred) Tj T*
(talents for a murrhine vase. The two acres \(_Morgen_\) of land which) Tj T*
(sufficed to the ancient citizens for a farm \(_Acker_\) were not now) Tj T*
(enough to make a fish-pond for imperial slaves. The sums carried by the) Tj T*
(exiles with them, to cover their traveling expenses and to live on for a) Tj T*
(time, were now greater than the fortunes of the most distinguished) Tj T*
(citizens had been in former times.[232-2] There was such a struggle) Tj T*
(among the people to surpass one another in procuring the freshest) Tj T*
(sea-fish that, at last, they would taste only such as they had seen) Tj T*
(alive on the table. We have the most exalted descriptions of the) Tj T*
(beautiful changes of color undergone by the dying fish; and a special) Tj T*
(infusion was invented to enable the epicure better to enjoy the) Tj T*
(spectacle.[232-3] Of the transparent garments of his time, Seneca says) Tj T*
(that they neither protected the body nor covered the nakedness of) Tj T*
(nature. People kept herds of sheep dyed in purple, although their) Tj T*
(natural white must have been much more agreeable to any one with an eye) Tj T*
(for the tasteful.[232-4] Not only on the roofs of houses were fish-ponds) Tj T*
(to be seen, but gardens even hanging on towers, and which must have been) Tj T*
(as small, ugly and inconvenient as they were costly.[232-5] Especially) Tj T*
(characteristic of the time was the custom of dissolving pearls in wine,) Tj T*
