(As in Italy, Frederick II., in Aragon, Iago I., in 1234, in England,) Tj T*
(Edward III., by 37, Edward III., c. 8 ff., so in France Philip IV. was) Tj T*
(the first who busied himself seriously with sumptuary legislation;[234-1]) Tj T*
(that is the same king who had introduced in so many things the modern) Tj T*
(political life into France. \(For instance, the ordinance of 1294,) Tj T*
(regulating apparel and the luxury of the table.\) In the 14th century, we) Tj T*
(find sumptuary laws directed mainly against expense for furs, and in the) Tj T*
(16th mainly against that for articles of gold and silver. From the) Tj T*
(descriptions left us in such laws of the prohibited luxuries, we may learn) Tj T*
(as much of the history of technology and of fashion, as we may of the) Tj T*
(history of classes from the gradation of the things permitted. The fines) Tj T*
(imposed for violations of these laws, under Philip IV. went for the most) Tj T*
(part to the territorial lord; and in the 16th and 17th centuries to the) Tj T*
(foundation of charitable institutions. The state, as a rule, took no share) Tj T*
(of them; doubtless to avoid the odium which might attach to this kind of) Tj T*
(revenue.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Beginning with the end of the 16th century, the sumptuary laws of France) Tj T*
(relating to the luxuries permitted to the several classes of the people) Tj T*
(disappear. The legislator ceases to be guided by moral considerations) Tj T*
(and begins to be influenced by reasons partaking of a commercial and) Tj T*
(police character; and here we may very clearly demonstrate the origin of) Tj T*
(the so-called mercantile or protective system. Thus, in the declaration) Tj T*
(of Louis XIV. dated December 12, 1644, we find a complaint, that not) Tj T*
(only does the importation of foreign articles of luxury threaten to rob) Tj T*
