(The impossibility of enforcing sumptuary laws has been most strikingly) Tj T*
(observed, where it has been attempted to suppress the consumption of) Tj T*
(popular delicacies in the first stages of their spread among the people.) Tj T*
(Thus, an effort was made in this direction in the sixteenth century, as) Tj T*
(regards brandy; in the seventeenth, as regards tobacco; in the) Tj T*
(eighteenth, as regards coffee; all which three articles were first) Tj T*
(allowed to be used only as medicines.[235-1] When governments discovered) Tj T*
(after some time the fruitlessness of the efforts, they gave up the) Tj T*
(prohibition of these luxuries and substituted taxes on them) Tj T*
(instead.[235-2] Thus an effort was made to combine a moral and a fiscal) Tj T*
(end. But it should not be lost sight of that the lower these taxes are,) Tj T*
(the greater the revenue they bring in; that is, the less the moral end) Tj T*
(is attained, the more is the fiscal end. Even Cato took this course. His) Tj T*
(office of censor, which united the highest moral superintendence with) Tj T*
(the highest financial guidance, must of itself have led him in this) Tj T*
(direction.[235-3] In modern times the most important excises and) Tj T*
(financial duties of entry have been evolved out of sumptuary laws. Even) Tj T*
(the Turks, after having long tried to prohibit tobacco-smoking in vain,) Tj T*
(afterwards found in the duties they imposed on that plant a rich source) Tj T*
(of income. That such taxes are among the best imposed, where they do not) Tj T*
(lead to frauds on the government, become excessive, or diminish) Tj T*
(consumption to too great an extent, is universally conceded.) Tj T*
