(SECTION CCXXXVII \(_b_\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(INSURANCE IN GENERAL.--ECONOMIC ADVANTAGES OF INSURANCE.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The national-economic advantage of insurance consists in this, that the) Tj T*
(damage which is divided among many, and which, therefore, is felt but) Tj T*
(lightly by each one, is probably made up for, not by an inroad upon the) Tj T*
(body of still existing original resources, but by savings made from) Tj T*
(income. This, indeed, is unconditionally true only of such damage as) Tj T*
(does not depend at all on the will of man, such as, for instance, the) Tj T*
(damage caused by hail. On the other hand, there is especially in) Tj T*
(maritime[237b-1] and fire insurance,[237b-2] a great temptation to) Tj T*
(culpable and even criminal destruction; to the latter, when the object) Tj T*
(insured is estimated at too high a value. \(Speculation-fires!\) And it is) Tj T*
(difficult to say whether this drawback or that advantage is the greater.) Tj T*
(But, on the other hand, every kind of insurance is attended by good) Tj T*
(consequences to the credit of a people. It is of advantage to personal) Tj T*
(credit, since it prevents sudden impoverishment; but it is by far more) Tj T*
(advantageous to real-credit \(_Realcredit_ = _material credit_\) the) Tj T*
(pledges of which, while their forms may be destroyed, it preserves the) Tj T*
(value of; that is their economic essence. This last is most clearly) Tj T*
(manifest in the case of public insurance institutions, with compulsory) Tj T*
(participation; while in the case of entirely voluntary insurance, the) Tj T*
(creditor can never be certain that his debtor has not neglected) Tj T*
(something necessary. The aggregate danger is less than the sum of) Tj T*
(individual dangers, for the reason that it is more certain, and that) Tj T*
(uncertainty of itself is an element of danger.[237b-3] [237b-4]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 237b-1: Even in Demosthenes' oration against) Tj T*
(     Zenothemis, we may see how easily the analogy of maritime) Tj T*
(     insurance may lead to criminal destruction of property.) Tj T*
(     Similar cases mentioned by _Pegolotti_ before the middle of) Tj T*
(     the 14th century. \(Delia Decima dei Fiorentini, III, 132.\)]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 237b-2: French experience teaches that during a) Tj T*
(     commercial crisis there are more fires in mercantile) Tj T*
(     magazines than at other times; while in times when sugar is) Tj T*
(     a drug in the market, etc., many sugar factories are burned.) Tj T*
(     \(Dictionnaire de l'Econ. polit, I, 88.\) The style of our) Tj T*
(     house-building and fire-extinguishing institutions is wont) Tj T*
(     to improve with economic culture. Hence, for instance, in) Tj T*
(     Mecklenburg, 1651 to 1799, cities burned down, in whole or) Tj T*
(     in greatest part, 72 times; 1800 to 1850, only once.) Tj T*
(     \(_Boll_, Gesch., von Mecklenb., II, 618 ff.\) However, in) Tj T*
(     many countries the damage caused by fire has largely) Tj T*
(     increased: in Baden, for instance, by 100,000 florins a) Tj T*
(     year. Insurance capital, 1809 to 1818, 65 fl.; 1819 to 1828,) Tj T*
(     128 fl.; 1829 to 1836, 152 fl. \(_Rau_, Archiv, III, 322.\)) Tj T*
(     Similarly in Switzerland. In Bavaria, of every 10,000) Tj T*
(     buildings insured, in 1856-60, there were 4.6 fires per) Tj T*
