(SECTION CCXXXVII \(_d_\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(REQUISITES OF A GOOD SYSTEM OF FIRE INSURANCE.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Among the chief requisites of a good fire insurance system are the) Tj T*
(following:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A. The adoption in insuring of measures for the prevention of criminal) Tj T*
(abuse on the part of the insured. No one should be benefited by the) Tj T*
(burning of his insured goods.[237d-1] Hence, the rates of insurance) Tj T*
(should be rigidly fixed according to the real value in exchange.[237d-2]) Tj T*
(In the case of houses, the value of the incombustible elements of value) Tj T*
(should be deducted; also the value of the ground and the value it) Tj T*
(possesses from being advantageously situated, etc. The simultaneous) Tj T*
(insurance of the same object in several companies without proper notice) Tj T*
(being given should be unconditionally prohibited.[237d-3] The control of) Tj T*
(all this may be greatly facilitated by requiring foreign insurance) Tj T*
(companies to obtain a special permit to carry on their business in the) Tj T*
(country, and to allow them to effect insurance only through responsible) Tj T*
(home agents.[237d-4] Most insurance companies exclude from insurance) Tj T*
(personal property which may be easily secreted, such, for instance, as) Tj T*
(jewels, cash money, valuable documents, etc.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(B. There should be a just proportion between the insurance premium and) Tj T*
(the risk. This depends not only on the style of building of the houses) Tj T*
(themselves and of those in the neighborhood,[237d-5] on the situation,) Tj T*
(the too great intricacy \(_Complicirung_\) of which extends the ravages of) Tj T*
(fire, as its too great isolation makes assistance difficult;[237d-6] but) Tj T*
