(SECTION CCXXXIX.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(LIMITS TO THE INCREASE OF POPULATION.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is certainly one limit which the increase of no organic being can) Tj T*
(exceed: the limit of the necessary means of subsistence. But, so far as) Tj T*
(the human race is concerned, this notion is somewhat more extensive,) Tj T*
(inasmuch as it embraces besides food, also clothing, shelter, fuel, and) Tj T*
(a great many other goods which are not, indeed, necessary to life, but) Tj T*
(which are so considered.[239-1] We may illustrate the matter by a simple) Tj T*
(example in the rule of division. If we take the aggregate of the means) Tj T*
(of subsistence as a dividend, the number of mankind as divisor; then the) Tj T*
(average share of each is the quotient. Where two of these quantities are) Tj T*
(given, the third may be found. Only when the dividend has largely) Tj T*
(increased can the divisor and quotient increase at the same time) Tj T*
(\(prosperous increase of population\). If, however, the quotient remains) Tj T*
(unchanged, the increase of the divisor can take place only at the) Tj T*
(expense of the quotient \(proletarian increase of population\).[239-2]) Tj T*
(Hence it is to be expected that the quantity of the means of subsistence) Tj T*
(being given and also the requirement of each individual, the number of) Tj T*
(births and the number of deaths should condition each other. Where, for) Tj T*
(instance, the number of church livings has not been increased, only as) Tj T*
(many candidates can marry as clergymen who held such livings have died.) Tj T*
(The greater the average age of the latter is, the later do the former) Tj T*
(marry, in the average, and _vice versa_. And so, in the case of whole) Tj T*
(nations, when their economic consumption and production remain) Tj T*
(unaltered.[239-3] A basin entirely filled with water can be made to) Tj T*
(contain more only in case it is either increased itself, or a means is) Tj T*
(found to compress its contents. Otherwise as much must flow out on the) Tj T*
(one side as is poured in on the other.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And so, everything else remaining stationary, the fruitfulness of) Tj T*
(marriages must, at least in the long run, be in the inverse ratio of) Tj T*
(their frequency. \(See § 247.\)[239-4] [239-5]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     [Footnote 239-1: When it is known that, in the Hebrides,) Tj T*
