(SECTION CCXLII.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(COUNTER TENDENCIES TO THE INCREASE OF POPULATION.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The extension of economic production is always a labor; the surrender of) Tj T*
(one's ordinary means of subsistence to new comers, a sacrifice; but, on) Tj T*
(the other hand, the procreation of children is a pleasure. Hence it) Tj T*
(seems to be incontestably true that the powers of increase of) Tj T*
(population, considered from an entirely sensuous point of view, tend to) Tj T*
(go beyond the bounds of the field of food. Malthus gave expression to) Tj T*
(this fact by saying that population had a tendency to increase in a) Tj T*
(geometrical progression, but the means of subsistence, even under the) Tj T*
(most favorable conditions, only in an arithmetical progression.[242-1]) Tj T*
(If the word "tendency" be correctly understood in the sense in which) Tj T*
(Malthus employed it, so that the reality appears as the product of) Tj T*
(several and partly opposite tendencies,[242-2] the first half of his) Tj T*
(allegation can scarcely be contested.[242-3] If a father has three sons,) Tj T*
(and each of the three three in turn, the love of procreation and the) Tj T*
(power of procreation, all being in the normal condition of health, are) Tj T*
(precisely three times as great in the second generation as in the first,) Tj T*
