(improvement in the means of subsistence, in hygienic measures, etc.,) Tj T*
(even for the lower classes, who everywhere constitute the great majority) Tj T*
(of the population. _Aisance est vitalité!_--at least on the supposition) Tj T*
(that morality remains the same.[246-6] How great may not have been the) Tj T*
(effect, for instance, of the healthier mode of the building of modern) Tj T*
(cities, of the disappearance of the greater number of fortifications) Tj T*
(etc., the more rational character of the healing art, the extension of) Tj T*
(vaccination,[246-7] the hygienic measures adopted by governments,[246-8]) Tj T*
(the better care of the poor and especially the asylums for small) Tj T*
(children! The modern system of agriculture and of the corn trade make) Tj T*
(famines less destructive of life.[246-9] \(§ 115\). The modern) Tj T*
(quarantine-system has protected us entirely against a number of plagues;) Tj T*
(and the worst epidemics of our day cannot be compared with those of) Tj T*
(earlier periods or in less civilized countries. In the second half of) Tj T*
(the 17th century, it was estimated in London that a plague would occur) Tj T*
(once in every 20 years, each of which swept away one-fifth of the entire) Tj T*
(population.[246-10] And in that very city the annual mortality between) Tj T*
(1740 and 1750 varied three-fifths, during the second half of the 18th) Tj T*
(century only one-third, during the 19th century only one-fifth in the) Tj T*
(same decade; a clear proof of the diminished fatality of) Tj T*
(epidemics.[246-11] [246-12]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
