(So far as the mere number of the population is concerned, it is) Tj T*
(obviously a matter of indifference whether there are annually 1,000) Tj T*
(births and 800 deaths, or 2,000 births and 1,800 deaths. But we see in) Tj T*
(the former an element of higher civilization,[248-1] especially, on) Tj T*
(account of the conditions which determine it. It can occur only where) Tj T*
(even the most numerous, that is the lower class, feel other wants than) Tj T*
(those of the mere means of existence and of the satisfaction of the) Tj T*
(sexual instinct: wants, duties which probably could not be satisfied in) Tj T*
(a state of marriage thoughtlessly entered into; where the virtues both) Tj T*
(of foresight and self-control are very generally practiced.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And then let us consider the consequences. The efficacy of the) Tj T*
(repressive hinderances to over-population either consists in immoral) Tj T*
(acts or easily leads to immorality. Until a "surplus" child has died,) Tj T*
(what a series of troubles for good parents, and what a chain of evil) Tj T*
(deeds for bad ones, to say nothing of the poor child itself.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Further, every man, no matter how short or long his life, requires a) Tj T*
(large advance of capital and trouble which he has later to return to) Tj T*
(society through the activity of his riper years. If he dies before his) Tj T*
(maturity, this advance has been made in vain. The more, therefore, the) Tj T*
(population of a country, in order to maintain itself within the bounds) Tj T*
(of its field of food, has to calculate on the death of children, the) Tj T*
(greater is this loss.[248-2] Hence, from a national-economic point of) Tj T*
(view, it is to be considered a great advance, that in England in 1780,) Tj T*
(there was one death among its people under 20 years of age in every 76) Tj T*
(of the population, in 1801, in every 96, in 1830, in every 124, in 1833,) Tj T*
(one only in every 137. \(_Porter._\) Lastly, the longer the average) Tj T*
(duration of life of a child the greater, other circumstances remaining) Tj T*
(the same, the number of grown people as compared with that of the) Tj T*
(children; but grown people are, as a rule, independent, capable of) Tj T*
(self-defense, economically productive, competent to discharge all the) Tj T*
(rights and duties of citizenship, while children are dependent,) Tj T*
(incapable of self-defense, unproductive, immature. Only he who knows the) Tj T*
(relative numbers of the different age-classes of a nation can draw) Tj T*
(fruitful conclusions from the data per capita relating to taxation, from) Tj T*
(the statistics of crime, suicides, illegitimate births, of) Tj T*
(school-children, etc., or judge correctly of a locality's military) Tj T*
(contingent.[248-3] [248-4] Here, indeed, it should not be overlooked that) Tj T*
(in the highest age-classes, human beings return in many respects to the) Tj T*
(helplessness of childhood. Yet, as a rule, to reach a good old age is) Tj T*
