(itself a productive force,[253-2] and of the utmost importance as a spur) Tj T*
(and as an auxiliary to the utilization of all other forces. The new is) Tj T*
(always attractive, by reason of its newness; but at the same time, we) Tj T*
(hold to the old too precisely because of its age: and the force of) Tj T*
(inertia would always turn the scales in favor of the latter. This) Tj T*
(inertia, both physical and mental is so general, that perhaps the) Tj T*
(majority of mankind would continue forever satisfied with their) Tj T*
(traditional field of occupation and with their traditional circle of) Tj T*
(food, were it not that an impulse as powerful and universal as the) Tj T*
(sexual and that of the love of children compelled them to extend the) Tj T*
(limits of both. That man might subdue the whole earth it was necessary) Tj T*
(that the Creator should make the tendency of man to multiply his kind) Tj T*
