(SECTION CCLIX.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(EFFECTS OF EMIGRATION.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(B. It is sufficiently evident that emigration from an over-populated) Tj T*
(country[259-1] may be attended with good consequences, especially when) Tj T*
(it takes place in organized bodies.[259-2] There is little danger that) Tj T*
(one who knows how to work and pray will go to the bad in a young) Tj T*
(agricultural colony. In a wilderness which has not yet been cleared, the) Tj T*
(greater number of proletarian vices spontaneously disappear. There is) Tj T*
(here no opportunity for jealousy or theft; little for intemperance, the) Tj T*
(gaming table, licentiousness or quarrelsomeness. Here labor is a) Tj T*
(necessity, and the rewards of industry and saving soon take a palpable) Tj T*
(shape. As the emigrant, in such a situation, can scarcely help marrying,) Tj T*
(children far from being a burthen, soon become companions to their) Tj T*
(parents in their solitude and, later, helpmates in business. The) Tj T*
(colonist belonging to the lower middle class is most certain of) Tj T*
(improving his condition. It may, indeed, require many and toilsome years) Tj T*
(before he can feel comfortable himself; but his children who would) Tj T*
(probably have led a proletarian life in the mother country may calculate) Tj T*
(with certainty on future well-being. The father's small capital which) Tj T*
(the outlay for education alone would have exhausted at home, here) Tj T*
(becomes the seed of a number of prosperous households.[259-3] It is) Tj T*
(otherwise with the mass of the people who remain at home. \(Compare §) Tj T*
(241.\)[259-4] It is a matter of much more difficulty than is generally) Tj T*
(supposed by those who have not made a study of the matter, that the) Tj T*
(yearly emigration from countries like Germany should counterbalance the) Tj T*
(excess of births over deaths.[259-5] It is not to be supposed that men) Tj T*
(who are really useless at home should be of any service in the colonies.) Tj T*
(How violently have not English colonies opposed the advent of settlers) Tj T*
(from the poorhouses of the mother country. The classes which are) Tj T*
(readiest to emigrate: idlers, fickle characters, fathers of families) Tj T*
(with altogether too many children, artisans who by a revolution in) Tj T*
(industry have lost the means of making a livelihood, are precisely those) Tj T*
(who find it most difficult to obtain employment on the other side of the) Tj T*
(water.[259-6] Most colonies refuse to receive persons over forty years) Tj T*
(of age at their own expense. But a young man intellectually and) Tj T*
(physically able to work, can always make his way even in the old world;) Tj T*
(only the weaker succumb under the pressure of over-population. Lastly,) Tj T*
(it should be considered what an amount of capital is required for) Tj T*
(purposes of emigration and settlement. If emigrants, on the average,) Tj T*
(take more capital with them than is estimated to be the _per capita_) Tj T*
(amount of capital possessed by those remaining at home,[259-7] the) Tj T*
(consequence would be that, as a result of this very successful) Tj T*
(emigration, the ratio of consumers to the amount of capital in the) Tj T*
(country would become more and more unfavorable. The emigrating portion) Tj T*
(of the country might experience the advantage of this, but the great) Tj T*
(mass of the population remaining at home would become poorer in capital) Tj T*
(and in vigorous men,[259-8] and richer in the comparatively needy. The) Tj T*
