(concern themselves; and considering the amount of education they) Tj T*
(generally possess, this need excite no surprise. Hence, if Germany would) Tj T*
(unite its departing children in a colony permanently German, and) Tj T*
(therefore new,[261-1] it would be necessary for it to offer them, at its) Tj T*
(own expense, at least the same advantages which they would find in older) Tj T*
(and fully established colonies. He who would reap should not endeavor to) Tj T*
(evade the sacrifice incident to the sowing.[261-2] Even great sacrifices) Tj T*
(in this direction would certainly be richly rewarded if properly made.) Tj T*
(Probably the outlay would never be directly returned to the national) Tj T*
(treasury; but there is all the more reason, on this account, that there) Tj T*
(should be an indirect return by the increase of duties and other) Tj T*
(indirect taxes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the other hand, the costly assistance of the state in the case of) Tj T*
(merely negative emigration would, as a rule, be folly. Who would compel) Tj T*
(the children of the great national family, who necessarily or) Tj T*
(voluntarily remain faithful to the paternal roof, to pay tribute to) Tj T*
(those who turn their backs on the old home for ever? The wealthy) Tj T*
(especially who remain in the country have to put up with the) Tj T*
(disadvantage of paying higher wages for labor.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Simple humanity requires that the state should not be blind to the) Tj T*
(movement of emigration, nor abandon it to all the risks of improvident) Tj T*
(liberty. Hence it should endeavor to remove the ignorance prevailing on) Tj T*
(questions of emigration. It should require personal and other guaranties) Tj T*
(that emigration agents are not simply dealers in men, and that the) Tj T*
(contracts made with ship-owners by emigrants are really performed. It) Tj T*
(should exercise a strict superintendence over the mode of transportation) Tj T*
(of emigrants, and see to it that its consuls accredited to America, etc.) Tj T*
(assist them by word and deed.[261-3] The legislation of Bremen is a) Tj T*
(model in this respect, and has contributed largely to make that port a) Tj T*
(principal outlet for German emigration.[261-4] The provisions of the) Tj T*
(laws of October 1, 1832, of July 14, 1854, of July 9, 1866, etc.,) Tj T*
(embrace among others the following: Only a citizen of Bremen, of good) Tj T*
(repute, and who has given security to the amount of five thousand) Tj T*
(thalers, shall be entitled to receive and contract with emigrants for) Tj T*
(passage; to each passenger shall be allotted a space of at least twelve) Tj T*
(square feet of surface and six feet high; provision shall be made for) Tj T*
(the longest possible time of passage; for instance, for thirteen weeks) Tj T*
