(THE MERCANTILE SYSTEM.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The principal peculiarities of the so-called mercantile system depend on) Tj T*
(a five-fold over-estimation: of the density of population, of the) Tj T*
(quantity of money, of foreign commerce, of the industries concerned with) Tj T*
(the transformation of materials \(_Verarbeitungsgewerbe_\), and of the) Tj T*
(guardianship of the state over private industry.[A2-1-1] All these) Tj T*
(tendencies are very intelligible, and almost self-evident, in a) Tj T*
(sovereign city-economy \(_Stadtwirthschaft_\) as opposed to the governed) Tj T*
(and worked-out \(_ausgebeuteten_\) country districts; as they are found) Tj T*
(even in the city-republics of later medieval times. But they are also) Tj T*
(natural in whole national economies, during that period of youthful and) Tj T*
(rapid growth in which the increasing density of population continues) Tj T*
(still, for a long time, to be really only a spur and an assistance, and) Tj T*
(in which, therefore, there can be no expression of anxiety concerning) Tj T*
(over-population; in which the new and rapidly growing division of labor) Tj T*
(draws attention particularly to the market-side of all businesses and to) Tj T*
(the circulation of goods; in which the progress from trade by barter to) Tj T*
(trade by money necessarily makes the volume of money needed even) Tj T*
(relatively greater; but especially are they natural in that world-period) Tj T*
(in which foreign trade suddenly increased enormously in consequence of) Tj T*
(the discovery of the whole earth; when the citizen classes of the people) Tj T*
(assumed immense importance as compared with the landed and clerical) Tj T*
(aristocracy, and when, in the internal affairs of state absolute) Tj T*
(monarchy, and in foreign politics, the system of equilibrium, through) Tj T*
(the instrumentality of the great compact-formation of states prevailed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(All these tendencies are most intimately connected with one another. If) Tj T*
(precious metal-money be really the essence of national wealth,[A2-1-2] a) Tj T*
(people who possess no gold and silver mines themselves;[A2-1-3] for) Tj T*
(instance, Italy, France and England, can become richer only through) Tj T*
(foreign trade,[A2-1-4] by means of a favorable balance produced by a) Tj T*
(preponderance of their exports over their imports; and only inasmuch as) Tj T*
(this excess is balanced by a payment in money from foreign parts. And) Tj T*
(so, too, in foreign trade, one nation can gain only what another nation) Tj T*
(has lost.[A2-1-5] Gain is promoted not only by direct obstacles placed) Tj T*
(in the way of the exportation of the precious metals, but still more by) Tj T*
(the value-enhancement of the exported commodities, and by the) Tj T*
(value-diminution of the imported commodities.[A2-1-6] And as commodities) Tj T*
(which have undergone the process of transformation are, on an average,) Tj T*
(more valuable than raw materials, the state can best carry out this) Tj T*
(policy by import duties, import prohibitions, and export premiums on) Tj T*
(manufactured articles, as well as by export duties, export prohibitions) Tj T*
(and import premiums on raw materials.[A2-1-7] This is extremely) Tj T*
(necessary against those nations who are superior to others in culture,) Tj T*
(wealth, the cheapness of labor and capital; and hence the envy of the) Tj T*
(mercantilists was directed chiefly against Holland, and after Colbert's) Tj T*
(time also against France.[A2-1-8] Such commodities as are not at all) Tj T*
(adapted to the nature of a country, because of its climate, for) Tj T*
