(The reaction against the mercantile theory of the balance of trade,) Tj T*
(which reached its height in Adam Smith, was based principally upon the) Tj T*
(following considerations:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A. Precious-metal-money is a commodity like all other commodities, and) Tj T*
(therefore useful only for certain purposes. It is as little to the) Tj T*
(wealth-interest of a people, by means of a continually favorable) Tj T*
(balance, to import infinite quantities of the precious metals, as it is) Tj T*
(to its power-interest, by means of its commercial policy, to accumulate) Tj T*
(infinite stores of powder. The person who possesses other exchangeable) Tj T*
(goods will be as well able, in case of need, to obtain gold and silver) Tj T*
(therewith as to obtain powder.[A2-2-1] We part with no capital when we) Tj T*
(export the precious metals and import other commodities instead; we) Tj T*
(simply exchange thereby one form of capital for another.[A2-2-2] The) Tj T*
(notion that the gain in trade is coincident with the balance of account) Tj T*
(paid in cash, is just as palpably false in the trade among nations as in) Tj T*
(trade among private persons.[A2-2-3] It would be a decided hardship to) Tj T*
(most men, if they were to receive payment at once in money for all that) Tj T*
