(THE INDUSTRIAL PROTECTIVE SYSTEM AND INTERNATIONAL FREE TRADE.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(SECTION I.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(PROXIMATE ECONOMIC EFFECTS OF THE INDUSTRIAL PROTECTIVE SYSTEM.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That the principal measures which the mercantile system recommended,) Tj T*
(artificially to increase a nation's wealth, could not produce the) Tj T*
(immediate effects expected of them, has been shown, especially from the) Tj T*
(natural history of money. Their proximate economic consequences) Tj T*
(necessarily consisted in this, that they diverted the existing) Tj T*
(productive forces of the nation from their places of application) Tj T*
(\(_Verwendungsplätzen_\) hitherto, to others which the government thought) Tj T*
(more advantageous.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A. If home producers are in a condition to offer their commodities as) Tj T*
(good and as cheap as foreigners, all protection of the former by import) Tj T*
(duties, or even by prohibitions, is superfluous. The home producer has,) Tj T*
(as a rule, not only the advantage of the smaller cost of freight to the) Tj T*
(place of consumption,[A3-1-1] but that of being earlier informed,) Tj T*
(because of his proximity to consumers, of a change in their) Tj T*
(tastes.[A3-1-2] If, indeed, foreigners could supply us better and) Tj T*
(cheaper, and if they are kept from supplying our market only by) Tj T*
(artificial means, the state compels our consumers to a sacrifice of) Tj T*
(enjoyment;[A3-1-3] and such a sacrifice as is not fully compensated for) Tj T*
(by the profit made by the favored producers in any manner. The latter) Tj T*
(are generally soon compelled by home competition to arrange their prices) Tj T*
(in accordance with the rate of profit usual in the country. If they had) Tj T*
(no "protection" they would simply employ their productive forces in) Tj T*
(other branches of production; and in those in which they were equal or) Tj T*
(even superior to foreign competitors. By means of the products thus) Tj T*
(obtained, the people might then get in exchange all those commodities) Tj T*
(from foreign countries, the production of which it is, according to the) Tj T*
(laws of the division of labor, better to leave to foreign) Tj T*
(countries.[A3-1-4] Since one nation can lastingly pay another nation) Tj T*
(only with its own products, any limitation of imports must, under) Tj T*
(otherwise equal circumstances, be attended by a corresponding limitation) Tj T*
(of exports.[A3-1-5] Directly, therefore, these hindrances to importation) Tj T*
(produce no increase, but only a change in the direction \(_Umlenkung_\) of) Tj T*
(the national forces of capital and labor; an increase, only in case that) Tj T*
(foreign producers are thereby caused to transfer their productive forces) Tj T*
(within our limits;[A3-1-6] which may certainly be considered the) Tj T*
(greatest triumph of the protective system. Hence it is absurd when an) Tj T*
(equal extension of "protection" to all the branches of a nation's) Tj T*
(economy is demanded, as it is so frequently, in the name of justice.) Tj T*
(There is here no real protection whatever, analogous, for instance, to) Tj T*
(the protection afforded by the judge, but a favor which can be accorded) Tj T*
(to no one without injuring some one else.[A3-1-7]) Tj T*
