(EFFECT OF EXPORT DUTIES, etc., ON RAW MATERIAL.--EXPORT PREMIUMS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(B. Export duties on raw material, and prohibitions of the exportation of) Tj T*
(raw material, lower the price of such articles, by preventing the) Tj T*
(competition of foreign buyers.[A3-2-1] To this loss of the producers of) Tj T*
(raw material, there is, in the long run, no corresponding gain to the) Tj T*
(manufacturers. Rather will there be, when freedom of competition) Tj T*
(prevails at home, an increased flow of the forces of production to the) Tj T*
(favored branch, because of its rate of profit, which is greater than) Tj T*
(that usual in the country, and a corresponding flow from the injured) Tj T*
(branch, until such time as the level of profit usual in the country is) Tj T*
(restored.[A3-2-2] Hence here, also, the final result is only a change of) Tj T*
(the direction, not a direct increase of the productive forces.[A3-2-3]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(C. In the case of export-premiums, it is necessary to distinguish) Tj T*
(between the mere refunding back of the taxes which have been paid on the) Tj T*
(assumption of a home consumption which has not taken place \(drawbacks\),) Tj T*
(and the actual making of donations because of the exportation of goods) Tj T*
(\(bounties\). The former produces no result except to maintain the) Tj T*
(possibility of a production which would otherwise have been prevented by) Tj T*
(the tax. The latter, on the contrary, compels all those who are subject) Tj T*
(to taxation to make a donation to one particular class of persons) Tj T*
(engaged in industry.[A3-2-4] Moreover, all consumers are compelled to) Tj T*
(pay a higher price for the commodity to the extent that the market) Tj T*
(price, inclusive of the premium to be obtained abroad, is higher than) Tj T*
(the home market price hitherto usual. But, as the cost of production has) Tj T*
(not increased, this profit of the producers, which is greater than that) Tj T*
(usual in the country, must induce other productive forces to enter into) Tj T*
(the favored branch; so that here, also, the lasting result is not a) Tj T*
(higher rate of profit of the individuals engaged in the industry, but an) Tj T*
(extension of the industry itself. Foreign countries chiefly reap the) Tj T*
(greatest advantage from this course, since they obtain the commodities) Tj T*
(at gift-prices.[A3-2-5] The premiums paid, not for exportation, but for) Tj T*
(the production of a commodity, have a meaning akin to this.[A3-2-6]) Tj T*
(Either the industry could not maintain itself without premiums, in which) Tj T*
(case the state encourages a losing production,--and the more there is) Tj T*
(produced the greater is the loss to the national economy;--or the) Tj T*
(industry might exist without the payment of premiums, and then the newly) Tj T*
(increased profit would lead to an extension of the industry. Exportation) Tj T*
(would follow, and all the effects of export-premiums appear.[A3-2-7]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
